  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface analysis this morning depicts a frontal boundary draped\nnorth of the forecast area that is progged to slip down from the\nnortheast during the day today. The lack of moisture associated\nwith the boundary and strong upper level ridging aloft will\nmitigate any measurable precipitation with this feature as it\nremains nestled nearby.\n\nAs the influence of the upper ridge gradually lessens this \nweek, a few subtleties to the forecast will ensue. With the \nfrontal boundary nearby today into Wednesday, an increase in \ncloud coverage will take shape. Once the boundary retreats to \nthe north as a warm front, the surface high will regain control \nfor the end of the work week and promote ample sunshine once \nmore. Additionally, post-frontal southwesterly flow will \nencourage a slight warm up that will mitigate any concern for \nfrost/freeze messaging, as overnight lows grow milder within \nthis regime. The ridge breaks down further into the weekend,\nwith a pattern change progged to take shape beginning on\nHalloween.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe predominant stretch of dry weather rounding out the second \nhalf of October comes to a conclusion this weekend as \nstrengthening low pressure across the western US takes aim for \nthe Ohio Valley. This will reintroduce measurable precipitation \nback into the forecast as early as late Saturday night into \nSunday morning, and maintaining unsettled weather through the \nremainder of the extended forecast period. Timing and extent of \nthis approaching disturbance will continue to be hashed out as \nwe get closer to the weekend timeframe.",
